{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Baikal Ice Riddle: Psychrophiles Beneath the Sworn Crystal",
    "date": "2026-08-21",
    "lesson": "Lake Baikal's winter ice cover (up to 2m thick) traps a unique freshwater microbial community in a cold, oxygen-rich, UV-flooded world unlike marine sea ice. Endemic diatoms (Aulacoseira baicalensis) bloom in a thin under-ice layer where meltwater channels let light through, while psychrophilic bacteria scavenge their exudates at near-zero temperatures. Because Baikal is freshwater and ultra-transparent, light penetrates far deeper than in turbid seas, and the ice itself is nearly salt-free, so there is no brine channel network the way there is in sea ice - just fine capillary cracks. This level asks: can a cold-adapted heterotroph make a living skimming diatom leakage through a nearly sterile ice ceiling before winter grazers (rotifers modeled as protists) intercept the bloom?",
    "citation": "Educational scenario inspired by studies of Lake Baikal under-ice diatom blooms and endemic psychrophilic bacterioplankton (e.g. Bondarenko et al., Mikhailov et al. limnology studies).",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Lake Baikal, Siberia - winter ice-covered pelagic zone, under-ice Aulacoseira baicalensis diatom bloom"
  },
